# {{.Name}}

An ultrastack product: business logic only, everything else is the paved road.
This file is the routing core. The framework's own SKILL.md (and its
`references/`) live in the ultrastack module; the `references/` here cover what
this product wires.

## The mental model

Constructors are providers; parameters are injections; implement
`Start`/`Stop`/`Healthy` and lifecycle is handled; wrap a parameter in
`Many`/`Lazy`/`Optional` for anything but exactly-one; every failure is a
structured diagnostic that names the fix. Roles are types, never string names.
The covenant: **if the wiring test passes, this app boots.**

## The verification loop (check, don't recall)

Hand-written prose drifts; the code does not. An agent that checks is immune to
its own hallucinations. Every time, in order:

1. **Before wiring** → `ultra vet ./...` (static DI checks before anything runs).
2. **On any `DIxxxx`** → `ultra explain <code>` — read the lesson; never fix
   from memory.
3. **Unsure of an API** → `go doc <pkg> <Symbol>` — never guess a signature.
4. **After changes** → `go test -run TestWiring ./...` (the covenant as CI).

## The rules of this repo

- `main.go` never grows. `Modules()` in `modules.go` is the whole assembly;
  business logic lives in modules — providers + `stack.Route`s + `di.Export`s.
- Every route declares its policy: `Require: "{{.Name}}.read"` (permission),
  `Require: "policy:name"` (custom Policy), or `Public: true`. The zero value
  means "any authenticated caller" — safe by default.
- Config = `config.toml` sections bound to typed structs
  (`conf.Section[T]("name")`); `[auth.roles]` hot-reloads. Env overrides:
  `ULTRA_<SECTION>_<FIELD>`. Print the reference: `go run . config reference`.

